    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d114">

                <pb facs="0203" n="173" sameAs="frus:frus1947v01/pg173"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> IO Files: US/A/392</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by Mr. G. Hayden Raynor of
                        the United States Delegation Staff of Advisers </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1947-09-17"
                            >September 17, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In the lounge during lunch today, I asked Mr. Falla<anchor n="1" xml:id="d114-f1"
                    /> if he knew the reaction of the United Kingdom Delegation to the Secretary’s
                        speech.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d114-f2"/> He was reserved in his reply,
                    stating that he had heard little <pb facs="0204" n="174" xml:id="pg174"/>
                    discussion of it but did, however, say that he personally questioned whether our
                    proposition for an Interim Committee of the Assembly was constitutional. I
                    explained to him that in our view it was clearly constitutional.</p>
                <p>Specifically, on the constitutional question, Mr. Falla asked how we could
                    overcome the definite concept in the Charter of the distinction between the
                    Assembly meeting only once a year and the Security Council being in continuous
                    session. He also expressed the thought that from the psychological point of view
                    we may be expected to have found less opposition if we had not termed the
                    proposal a committee on peace and security—if we had simply said interim
                    committee of the General Assembly and eliminated the words peace and security
                    which he thought brought out into sharp focus the possible conflict of
                    jurisdiction with the Security Council.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d114-f1" n="1" xml:id="d114-f1-content">
                <p>Paul S. Falla, United Kingdom Delegation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d114-f2" n="2" xml:id="d114-f2-content">
                <p>Regarding the Secretary of State’s address to the General Assembly on September
                    17, see the editorial note, <ref target="#pg14">p. 14</ref>.</p>
            </note>
